{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"pointers","title":"Pointers","group":"Pointers, Arrays & Strings","order":19,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A pointer is a variable that holds the <em>address</em> of an object. <code>&amp;x</code> takes an address, <code>*p</code> follows it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Pointer</strong> — an object of type <code>T*</code> whose value is the memory address of a <code>T</code> (or <code>nullptr</code>). The pointer and the object it points to are separate — each can be <code>const</code> independently.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int x = 10;\n    int* p = &amp;x;          // p holds x's address\n    *p = 20;              // write through the pointer\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (p == &amp;x) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int y = 7;\n    p = &amp;y;               // re-point to another object\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int** pp = &amp;p;        // pointer to pointer\n    **pp = 8;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; y &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(p) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // every data pointer is 8 bytes on 64-bit\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>20 20 1\n7\n8 8</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int* p;</code></td><td>Declare <code>p</code> as pointer to <code>int</code> (uninitialised — dangerous)</td></tr><tr><td><code>int* p = nullptr;</code></td><td>Null pointer — points to nothing</td></tr><tr><td><code>&amp;x</code></td><td>Address of <code>x</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*p</code></td><td>The object <code>p</code> points to (dereference)</td></tr><tr><td><code>p-&gt;m</code></td><td>Member <code>m</code> of the pointed-to object = <code>(*p).m</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>p + n</code></td><td>Address <code>n</code> <em>elements</em> further (scaled by <code>sizeof(T)</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>p[i]</code></td><td><code>*(p + i)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Pointer arithmetic &amp; arrays</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n    int* p = arr;                 // array decays to a pointer to its first element\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *(p + 2) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p[3] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    int* end = arr + 5;           // one-past-the-end: may be compared, never dereferenced\n    for (int* it = arr; it != end; ++it) std::cout &lt;&lt; *it / 10;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n' &lt;&lt; (end - p) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // difference = number of elements (ptrdiff_t)\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>30 40\n12345\n5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-off\"></i><code>nullptr</code> vs <code>NULL</code> vs <code>0</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nvoid f(int)   { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"f(int)\\n\"; }\nvoid f(int*)  { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"f(int*)\\n\"; }\nint main() {\n    f(0);          // 0 is an int → f(int)\n    f(nullptr);    // nullptr has its own type → f(int*)\n    int* p = nullptr;\n    if (!p) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"null\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>f(int)\nf(int*)\nnull</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Always use <code>nullptr</code> (C++11). <code>NULL</code> is a macro for <code>0</code> (or <code>0L</code>) and picks integer overloads.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pointer dangers</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circle-off\"></i>Null dereference</h3><p><code>*p</code> when <code>p == nullptr</code> — crash (undefined behaviour). Check before use.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-unlink\"></i>Dangling pointer</h3><p>Points to an object that was destroyed (freed heap memory, a local that went out of scope).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>Wild pointer</h3><p>Never initialised — holds garbage. Always initialise pointers.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-maximize\"></i>Out of bounds</h3><p>Arithmetic past the array — reads/writes someone else's memory.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — dangling pointer (ASan catches it)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int* p = nullptr;\n    {\n        int local = 5;\n        p = &amp;local;\n    }                         // local destroyed here — p now dangles\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; '\\n';  // AddressSanitizer: stack-use-after-scope\n}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i><code>const</code> and pointers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Can change <code>*p</code>?</th><th>Can change <code>p</code>?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int* p</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>const int* p</code> (= <code>int const* p</code>)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>int* const p</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>const int* const p</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between a pointer and a reference?</summary><div><p>A pointer is an object holding an address: it can be null, re-pointed, and has its own address; you dereference it explicitly. A reference is an <em>alias</em> for an existing object: it must be initialised, can't be null or re-seated, and is used with ordinary syntax. Prefer references for parameters; use pointers when “no object” or re-pointing is needed.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the size of a pointer?</summary><div><p>It depends on the platform, not the pointee: 8 bytes on 64-bit systems, 4 on 32-bit. <code>sizeof(char*) == sizeof(double*)</code>. (Pointers to members can be larger.)</p></div></details></section>"}